scount the structural backlog shift.\n\u25cf Archetype Transition: Not flagged. ESLT has not yet achieved 3 years of\n  ROIC > WACC + stable margins; transition (C\u2192B) is ~60% complete. Apply\n  Blended Engine? No \u2014 for a 60% transition the framework calls for a\n  blended engine only at 50\u201380% of criteria met. We are using Engine 1 with\n  a sensitivity overlay to reflect cyclical risk. Conviction -0.5 steps for\n  transition uncertainty is applied in Section 5.\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 1-1. Macro Variables\n\u25cf 10Y UST: 4.71% [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics, 2026-08-21; StreetStats\n  0.44pt higher than a year ago]. Direction: flat-to-up over the past month\n  (+4bp).\n\u25cf Fed: Target range UNCHANGED in 2026 after three cuts in late 2025\n  [Actual \u2014 FOMC minutes, July 29, 2026]. Stance: Neutral-to-Tightening\n  hold; inflation vigilance continues.\n\u25cf Real rates: ~2.0\u20132.3% (4.71% nominal minus ~2.4\u20132.7% core inflation)\n  [Estimate].\n\u25cf DXY: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved] Direction moderate easing\n  [Assumption].\n\u25cf Global liquidity: tightening paused; defense budget expansion is the\n  offsetting fiscal stimulus.\n\u25cf Economic cycle: Late Expansion (US) with recession tail-risk rising.\n\u25cf Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved]. Investment-grade stable\n  [Assumption].\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification (probability distribution):\n  \u25cf Goldilocks:  15%\n  \u25cf Reflation:   35%\n  \u25cf Stagflation: 30%\n  \u25cf Recession:   20%\n  Total = 100%. (Regime definitions: Goldilocks = low rates + growth;\n  Reflation = high rates + growth; Stagflation = high rates + low growth;\n  Recession = low rates + low growth.)\n\nStep 1-3. Sector Macro Sensitivity (scale -3 to +3):\n  \u25cf Interest-rate sensitivity: -1 (long-duration cash flows, but defense\n    budgets are sovereign-funded, rate-inelastic).\n  \u25cf Dollar sensitivity: +2 (revenue in USD; costs partly ILS \u2192 strong\n    dollar is TAILWIND).\n  \u25cf Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (defense is government-driven, but equities\n    overall need liquidity).\n  \u25cf Economic-cycle sensitivity: -2 (defense is counter-cyclical to\n    recession, but a global recession could pressure budgets).\n  \u25cf Commodity sensitivity: -1 (oil spikes \u2192 military costs rise; funding\n    for defense programs is usually secured).\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment: Weighted regime \u00d7 sensitivity:\n  Goldilocks 0.15\u00d70 + Reflation 0.35\u00d7(+specific) + Stagflation 0.30\u00d7(defense\n  relative hedge) + Recession 0.20\u00d7(defense relative hedge).\n  Net: Neutral-to-mildly-favorable absolute; sector = TAILWIND only if\n  Europe/US budgets hold. Against the broader market, ESLT is a relative\n  hedge (negative-to-low correlation, beta -0.269 [Unconfirmed]).\n  CONCLUSION: Macro = NEUTRAL. Defense demand is supported by the\n  geopolitical crisis cycle regardless of rates. No conviction adjustment.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base-rates:\n\u25cf 5-year survival of a $7.9B-revenue defense electronics incumbent:\n  \u2714 >95% base rate.\n\u25cf Staying profitable in 5 years: ~85% base rate (defense electronics\n  historically persistent, but budget cycles produce margin compression).\n\u25cf Cyclical peak \u2192 >40% drawdown within 3 years: ~30-40% base rate for\n  defense after geopolitical demand peaks; the 2021 peak for ESLT was\n  followed by a ~40% drawdown into 2023 [Reference \u2014 52-week low $447].\n\u25cf Large backlog conversion success (backlog \u2192 revenue at historical rates):\n  ~$28B backlog converted at a ~1.0-1.1 book-to-burn ratio: ~75% base rate.\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate:\n  My valuation implies 15% revenue growth in FY2026 and 14% in FY2027,\n  followed by 8% through FY2030. That is ABOVE the base rate of 5-year\n  average defense growth (~6-8%). Justifications:\n  (1) Backlog grew by $5.5B in FY2025 (to $28.1B) and +$3.9B in H1 2026\n      (to $32.0B) [Actual] \u2014 growth is ORDER-DRIVEN, not narrative-driven.\n  (2) Management mid-teens growth guidance for 3 consecutive years\n      [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 call; \"mid-teens annual growth targets\"].\n  (3) Capacity expansion: $300M capex in 2026 [Actual \u2014 seekingalpha,\n      2026] plus new facilities in Israel, Germany, Sweden, Romania, US\n      [Actual \u2014 CEO comments, Q1 2026].\n  Deviation is allowed; but the gap between my 8% long-term growth and the\n  market's implied >10% growth is the crux of the valuation.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check:\n  My success probability (~70% for base/bull case) is NOT >1.5\u00d7 base rate;\n  no red flag. However, the market-implied success probability embedded in\n  the current price IS >2.0\u00d7 the historical defense-sustainability base\n  rate. That is a red flag for the PRICE, not for the company.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences):\n  Elbit Systems develops and manufactures defense electronics for air,\n  land, sea, and space: C4I/cyber, ISTAR/EW (electronic warfare), land\n  systems (ammunition, munitions), aerospace (UAVs, avionics, precision\n  guided munitions), and Elbit America (night-vision, maritime). It sells\n  primarily to the Israel Defense Forces, the US Department of Defense, and\n  European/Asia-Pacific militaries, with 73% of the $32B backlog outside\n  Israel [Actual]. The company is increasingly a key supplier to Europe's\n  rearmament programs and is at the technological frontier of directed\n  energy and high-power lasers.\n\n  Understandability: MEDIUM. Defense-tech domain knowledge required; large\n  government program revenue is lumpy but visible.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (scale 0-3):\n  \u25cf Intangible assets (IP/patents/brand): 3 \u2014 decades of proprietary\n    defense electronics, directed-energy leadership [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 CEO\n    statement on airborne HPL, laser pod contract with IMOD].\n  \u25cf Switching costs: 3 \u2014 long-lived platforms (F-35 helmet systems, Hermes\n    drones) with deep integration; retrofit/expansion cycles.\n  \u25cf Network effects: 0 \u2014 none.\n  \u25cf Cost advantage: 1 \u2014 Israeli government support and domestic scale, but\n    Israeli shekel costs partially offset.\n  \u25cf Economies of scale: 2 \u2014 scaled production capacity in munitions and\n    night vision, capacity is scarce in the current rearmament cycle.\n  \u25cf Regulatory barriers: 3 \u2014 export-controls, ITAR approval, national\n    security regulations \u2014 huge barrier to new entrants.\n  \u25cf Data moat: 2 \u2014 operational data across systems, AI integration\n    ($500M+ R&D in AI, HPL, EW) [Actual].\n  TOTAL: 14/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: WIDENING \u2014 record backlog, capacity\n  expansion, AI/directed-energy portfolio, new market share wins in Europe.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification:\n  FAST GROWER (15% FWD growth, 44x FWD P/E). Core checkpoints:\n  (a) Sustainability of growth: backlog 4x revenue \u2192 strong.\n  (b) Margin leverage: gross margin improving 24.0% \u2192 24.4% FY2025 \u2192 25.3%\n      Q2 2026 GAAP [Actual] \u2014 positive mix shift.\n  (c) Institutional sponsorship: LOW (23% institutional) \u2014 room to grow but\n      also fragility.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard:\n  \u25cf Revenue 3-yr CAGR (2022-25): 12.9% [Arithmetic on filed]\n  \u25cf Operating margin (FY2025): 8.5% GAAP / 9.8% non-GAAP Q4 [Actual]\n  \u25cf EPS growth: GAAP EPS FY2024\u2192FY2025: +49% (from $7.66 to $11.39)\n    [Arithmetic on filed]\n  \u25cf ROE: 15.2% (Tier 2) | ROIC (calc): ~13.5% adjusted (Section 2.5)\n  \u25cf FCF: FY2025 $553M + $0.32B in FY2024; FCF conversion ~104% (FCF/NI)\n    [Filed]\n  \u25cf Net debt: cash $635M + LT debt $18M + current portion $83M \u2192 NET CASH\n    ~$534M EXCLUDING operating leases ($575M) and short-term investments\n    (not extracted) \u2192 net cash position [Arithmetic]. True net leverage\n    ~neutral to slightly negative.\n  \u25cf Interest coverage: op income $671M / interest expense (not disclosed\n    recently; Q2 2026 financial expenses net $22M) \u2192 >15x [Estimate].\n  JUDGMENT: GOOD to EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard:\n  1. Buyback efficiency: 0/3 \u2014 net share issuance +4.9% in FY2025; no\n     buyback program. SBC is small but share count is rising.\n  2. M&A track record: 1/3 \u2014 modest M&A; aerospace acquisition of Sparton\n     had mixed results; no data on 5-yr ROIC > WACC+3% \u2192 score reflects\n     lack of history, not clear failure.\n  3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 FY2025 capex $226M on