n  \u25cf Reflation:   35%\n  \u25cf Stagflation: 30%\n  \u25cf Recession:   20%\n  Total = 100%. (Regime definitions: Goldilocks = low rates + growth;\n  Reflation = high rates + growth; Stagflation = high rates + low growth;\n  Recession = low rates + low growth.)\n\nStep 1-3. Sector Macro Sensitivity (scale -3 to +3):\n  \u25cf Interest-rate sensitivity: -1 (long-duration cash flows, but defense\n    budgets are sovereign-funded, rate-inelastic).\n  \u25cf Dollar sensitivity: +2 (revenue in USD; costs partly ILS \u2192 strong\n    dollar is TAILWIND).\n  \u25cf Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (defense is government-driven, but equities\n    overall need liquidity).\n  \u25cf Economic-cycle sensitivity: -2 (defense is counter-cyclical to\n    recession, but a global recession could pressure budgets).\n  \u25cf Commodity sensitivity: -1 (oil spikes \u2192 military costs rise; funding\n    for defense programs is usually secured).\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment: Weighted regime \u00d7 sensitivity:\n  Goldilocks 0.15\u00d70 + Reflation 0.35\u00d7(+specific) + Stagflation 0.30\u00d7(defense\n  relative hedge) + Recession 0.20\u00d7(defense relative hedge).\n  Net: Neutral-to-mildly-favorable absolute; sector = TAILWIND only if\n  Europe/US budgets hold. Against the broader market, ESLT is a relative\n  hedge (negative-to-low correlation, beta -0.269 [Unconfirmed]).\n  CONCLUSION: Macro = NEUTRAL. Defense demand is supported by the\n  geopolitical crisis cycle regardless of rates. No conviction adjustment.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base-rates:\n\u25cf 5-year survival of a $7.9B-revenue defense electronics incumbent:\n  \u2714 >95% base rate.\n\u25cf Staying profitable in 5 years: ~85% base rate (defense electronics\n  historically persistent, but budget cycles produce margin compression).\n\u25cf Cyclical peak \u2192 >40% drawdown within 3 years: ~30-40% base rate for\n  defense after geopolitical demand peaks; the 2021 peak for ESLT was\n  followed by a ~40% drawdown into 2023 [Reference \u2014 52-week low $447].\n\u25cf Large backlog conversion success (backlog \u2192 revenue at historical rates):\n  ~$28B backlog converted at a ~1.0-1.1 book-to-burn ratio: ~75% base rate.\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate:\n  My valuation implies 15% revenue growth in FY2026 and 14% in FY2027,\n  followed by 8% through FY2030. That is ABOVE the base rate of 5-year\n  average defense growth (~6-8%). Justifications:\n  (1) Backlog grew by $5.5B in FY2025 (to $28.1B) and +$3.9B in H1 2026\n      (to $32.0B) [Actual] \u2014 growth is ORDER-DRIVEN, not narrative-driven.\n  (2) Management mid-teens growth guidance for 3 consecutive years\n      [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 call; \"mid-teens annual growth targets\"].\n  (3) Capacity expansion: $300M capex in 2026 [Actual \u2014 seekingalpha,\n      2026] plus new facilities in Israel, Germany, Sweden, Romania, US\n      [Actual \u2014 CEO comments, Q1 2026].\n  Deviation is allowed; but the gap between my 8% long-term growth and the\n  market's implied >10% growth is the crux of the valuation.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check:\n  My success probability (~70% for base/bull case) is NOT >1.5\u00d7 base rate;\n  no red flag. However, the market-implied success probability embedded in\n  the current price IS >2.0\u00d7 the historical defense-sustainability base\n  rate. That is a red flag for the PRICE, not for the company.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding (3 sentences):\n  Elbit Systems develops and manufactures defense electronics for air,\n  land, sea, and space: C4I/cyber, ISTAR/EW (electronic warfare), land\n  systems (ammunition, munitions), aerospace (UAVs, avionics, precision\n  guided munitions), and Elbit America (night-vision, maritime). It sells\n  primarily to the Israel Defense Forces, the US Department of Defense, and\n  European/Asia-Pacific militaries, with 73% of the $32B backlog outside\n  Israel [Actual]. The company is increasingly a key supplier to Europe's\n  rearmament programs and is at the technological frontier of directed\n  energy and high-power lasers.\n\n  Understandability: MEDIUM. Defense-tech domain knowledge required; large\n  government program revenue is lumpy but visible.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (scale 0-3):\n  \u25cf Intangible assets (IP/patents/brand): 3 \u2014 decades of proprietary\n    defense electronics, directed-energy leadership [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 CEO\n    statement on airborne HPL, laser pod contract with IMOD].\n  \u25cf Switching costs: 3 \u2014 long-lived platforms (F-35 helmet systems, Hermes\n    drones) with deep integration; retrofit/expansion cycles.\n  \u25cf Network effects: 0 \u2014 none.\n  \u25cf Cost advantage: 1 \u2014 Israeli government support and domestic scale, but\n    Israeli shekel costs partially offset.\n  \u25cf Economies of scale: 2 \u2014 scaled production capacity in munitions and\n    night vision, capacity is scarce in the current rearmament cycle.\n  \u25cf Regulatory barriers: 3 \u2014 export-controls, ITAR approval, national\n    security regulations \u2014 huge barrier to new entrants.\n  \u25cf Data moat: 2 \u2014 operational data across systems, AI integration\n    ($500M+ R&D in AI, HPL, EW) [Actual].\n  TOTAL: 14/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: WIDENING \u2014 record backlog, capacity\n  expansion, AI/directed-energy portfolio, new market share wins in Europe.\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification:\n  FAST GROWER (15% FWD growth, 44x FWD P/E). Core checkpoints:\n  (a) Sustainability of growth: backlog 4x revenue \u2192 strong.\n  (b) Margin leverage: gross margin improving 24.0% \u2192 24.4% FY2025 \u2192 25.3%\n      Q2 2026 GAAP [Actual] \u2014 positive mix shift.\n  (c) Institutional sponsorship: LOW (23% institutional) \u2014 room to grow but\n      also fragility.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard:\n  \u25cf Revenue 3-yr CAGR (2022-25): 12.9% [Arithmetic on filed]\n  \u25cf Operating margin (FY2025): 8.5% GAAP / 9.8% non-GAAP Q4 [Actual]\n  \u25cf EPS growth: GAAP EPS FY2024\u2192FY2025: +49% (from $7.66 to $11.39)\n    [Arithmetic on filed]\n  \u25cf ROE: 15.2% (Tier 2) | ROIC (calc): ~13.5% adjusted (Section 2.5)\n  \u25cf FCF: FY2025 $553M + $0.32B in FY2024; FCF conversion ~104% (FCF/NI)\n    [Filed]\n  \u25cf Net debt: cash $635M + LT debt $18M + current portion $83M \u2192 NET CASH\n    ~$534M EXCLUDING operating leases ($575M) and short-term investments\n    (not extracted) \u2192 net cash position [Arithmetic]. True net leverage\n    ~neutral to slightly negative.\n  \u25cf Interest coverage: op income $671M / interest expense (not disclosed\n    recently; Q2 2026 financial expenses net $22M) \u2192 >15x [Estimate].\n  JUDGMENT: GOOD to EXCELLENT.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard:\n  1. Buyback efficiency: 0/3 \u2014 net share issuance +4.9% in FY2025; no\n     buyback program. SBC is small but share count is rising.\n  2. M&A track record: 1/3 \u2014 modest M&A; aerospace acquisition of Sparton\n     had mixed results; no data on 5-yr ROIC > WACC+3% \u2192 score reflects\n     lack of history, not clear failure.\n  3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 FY2025 capex $226M on OCF $778M, incremental\n     ROIC good; IF $300M expansion investments yield guided growth, this\n     deserves 3.\n  4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 \u2014 capex + dividend ($4.00/yr) modest; no\n     buyback while issuing shares \u2192 slight mark-down.\n  5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 \u2014 Q2 2026 EPS beat consensus by 27.8%; Q1\n     2026 beat by ~36%; FY2025 performance above internal targets.\n  Insider bonus: +1 (CEO/high mgmt ownership 41.23% [Tier 2]), no insider\n  sell clusters.\n  TOTAL: 9/15 + 1 = 10/16 \u2192 GOOD.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization:\n  FY2025 R&D = $517.1M [Actual \u2014 FY2025 release]. Capitalize 50% ($258M),\n  amortize over 4 years \u2192 add $65M/yr amortization to op income; add\n  $193M net to NOPAT. Adjusted op income \u2248 $864M (from $671M + $193M).\n  Adjusted invested capital: equity $4,130M + net debt incl. leases $575M\n  - cash $635M + debt $18M + current $83M = $4,171M + unamortized R&D\n  $258M = $4,429M.\n  ADJUSTED ROIC \u2248 ($864M \u00d7 0.835 tax adj) / $4,429M \u2248 $721M/$4,429M = 16.3%.\n  GAAP ROIC \u2248 13.5%. Spread = +2.8pp \u2014 modest, no distortion.\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC:\n  FY2025 SBC $26M [Filed] \u2248 0.33% of revenue. Adjusted FCF = Rptd FCF\n  $553M - $26M = $527M. SBC is NOT a major drain. Share-count growth\n  driven by unknown issuance (possibly acquisition / capital raise for\n  Israel expansion) [Unconfirmed].\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization:\n  Operating leases $575M (FY2025) [Filed]. Adjust net debt +$575M:\n  Adjusted net debt/EBITDA \u2248 ($575M - $635M cash + $101M debt) / $850M\n  EBITDA \u2248 0.05x \u2192 effectively net-cash position. No credit concern.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data Asset:\n  Defense brand value implied by pricing power (night-vision, EW platforms)\n  [Estimate]. Data/AI assets not separately valued; included in R&D.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: 16.3% [Estimate]. Gap vs GAAP = 2.8pp, below\n  5pp threshold \u2192 no further correction needed.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett):\n  Owner Earnings = NI $534M + D&A $171M - Maintenance Capex (~$70M\n  [Estimate ~1% of revenue]) - \u0394WC (2025 +$715M \u2014 working capital is a\n  HEAVY consumer; partly funded by customer advances) = ~$650M to $920M\n  excluding WC; with full WC: \u2212$80M. This explains why FCF is lumpy.\n  Use reported FCF $553M [Actual] as the operational cash-flow reference.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1: DCF + MULTIPLES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n3-A. Forecast Drivers (all sourced)\n\nRevenue growth:\n  \u25cf FY2026: +15.0% \u2014 management mid-teens guidance [Actual], consistent\n    with Q1 +Q2 run-rate ($2.19B + $2.287B = H1 $4.48B + H2\n    [Assumption]).\n  \u25cf FY2027: +14.0% \u2014 guided mid-teens for 2026 only; beyond that,\n    backlog scheduled performance: 42% of $32B = $13.4B through 2027\n    [Actual] implies >$13B revenue over 2026-27; 14% is supported.\n  \u25cf FY2028: +10% | FY2029: +8% | FY2030: +8% \u2014 [Assumption] \u2014 European\n    rearmament cycle and NATO >3.5% GDP targets support 5-year growth;\n    no official guidance beyond 2027. The range of outcomes is wide:\n    +10% (super-cycle persists) to +4% (budget normalization).\n\nOperating margin (NON-GAAP, adjusted):\n  \u25cf FY2026: 10.2% \u2014 Q2 2026 non-GAAP op margin 10.4% [Actual] and\n    management \"surpassing internal targets.\" Use slightly conservative\n    10.2%.\n  \u25cf FY2027: 10.8% | FY2028: 11.2% | FY2029: 11.4% | FY2030: 11.5% \u2014\n    [Assumption] consistent with management's stated \"margin expansion\"\n    and historical gross-margin progression (24.0\u219225.3% over 4 quarters).\n\nTax rate:\n  \u25cf FY2026: 16.5%; FY2027-28: 17%; thereafter 17% [Assumption/Actual \u2014\n    Q2 2026 effective rate 16.4% due to OECD Pillar II [Actual]].\n  \u25cf FY2025 full-year GAAP tax rate was ~13-14% [Computed with caution as\n    pack fields are broken; use 16-17% normalized].\n\nCapex:\n  \u25cf FY2026: $300M [Actual \u2014 management H1 2026 plan: \"$300 million\n    capital expenditure plan for 2026\"].\n  \u25cf FY2027-2030: 2.5-2.7% of revenue (\u2248$260-300M) [Estimate].\n\nD&A:\n  \u25cf FY2026-2030: 2.2-2.4% of revenue ($190-270M) [Assumption on prior\n    ratios ~2.2-2.5%].\n\n\u0394Working capital:\n  \u25cf ~2% of incremental revenue (receivables/inventory growth offset by\n    advances/contract liabilities) [Assumption]. FY2025 WC consumed $715M\n    [Arithmetic]; contract-liability build was heavy; model net modest\n    drag.\n\nSBC:\n  \u25cf Included as expense in operating margin (non-GAAP) [Assumption].\n\n3-B. Discount-Rate Derivation [Assumption-heavy]\n  \u25cf Risk-free rate: 4.71% [Actual \u2014 10Y UST, TradingEconomics 2026-08-21]\n  \u25cf ERP: 4.75% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran-style range 4.5-5.0%]\n  \u25cf Equity beta: 0.85 [Assumption; REJECT the vendor beta of -0.269 as\n    unreliable [Unconfirmed] \u2014 a -0.27 beta implies near-zero equity risk,\n    inconsistent with Israel geopolitics and defense-cycle risk].\n  \u25cf Country-risk premium (Israel): +0.50% [Assumption \u2014 heightened\n    sovereign risk, Iran-war tail].\n  \u25cf Cost of equity = 4.71% + 0.85\u00d74.75% + 0.50% = 9.25%\n  \u25cf Cost of debt (pre-tax): 5.5% [Assumption, A-/Baa credit]; after-tax\n    at 16.5% = 4.60%\n  \u25cf Debt weight: 12% (incl. leases) [Arithmetic: $101M debt + 575M leases\n    vs. equity $4,130M]\n  \u25cf WACC = 0.88\u00d79.25% + 0.12\u00d74.60% = 8.14% + 0.55% = 8.69% \u2192 ROUND to\n    8.5% base, with sensitivity at 8.0% and 9.5%.\n\n3-C. Explicit DCF (Base Case, WACC 8.5%, g=4.0%)\n\n  $M           2026     2027     2028     2029     2030\n  Revenue      9,130   10,405   11,445   12,361   13,350\n  Growth        15.0%    14.0%    10.0%     8.0%     8.0%\n  Op. margin    10.2%    10.8%    11.2%    11.4%    11.5%\n  Op income      931    1,124    1,282    1,409    1,535\n  Tax @ 16.5/17% (154)   (185)    (218)    (240)    (261)\n  NOPAT          777      938    1,064    1,170    1,274\n  +D&A          ~200     ~215     ~235     ~255     ~280\n  -Capex        (300)    (280)    (275)    (290)    (300)\n  -\u0394WC           (25)     (26)     (21)     (18)     (20)\n  FCF            652      847    1,003    1,117    1,234\n\n  Discount factor @8.5%: 0.9217  0.8495  0.7829  0.7216  0.6650\n  PV of FCF:                 601     720     785     806     821\n  Sum PV FCFs (5-yr) = 3,733\n  Terminal value = (1,234 \u00d7 1.04) / (0.085 \u2212 0.04) = 28,530\n  PV of TV = 28,530 \u00d7 0.6650 = 18,972\n  EV = 3,733 + 18,972 = 22,705\n  + Cash 635 \u2212 LT debt 18 \u2212 current portion 83 \u2212 short-term borrowings\n    (not extracted; 0 in FY2025) + investments [Unconfirmed, omitted]\n    \u2212 leases (0 \u2014 leases already in WACC debt weight) [Method note]\n  Net cash = 534\n  Equity value = 23,239\n  Shares = 46.918M [Filed, FY2025] [Unverified \u2014 no 10-K row; treat as\n  best available]\n  **IV (Base) = $495 / share**\n\n3-D. Sensitivity to WACC & terminal g (after the guided period):\n\n           g=3.5%    g=4.0%    g=4.5%\n  WACC 8.0%   $556      $620      $705\n  WACC 8.5%   $452      $495      $552\n  WACC 9.5%   $340      $365      $397\n\n  \u21d2 The valuation beyond FY2027 (where I have actual backlog) is heavily\n  assumption-dependent. Plausible range: **$340 \u2013 $705 / share**; central\n  estimate **$495**.\n\n3-E. Multiples Cross-Check (against 2026E):\n  \u25cf Forward P/E (2026E non-GAAP EPS ~$16.5 [Estimate]): current\n    $741.33/$16.5 = 44.9x; base-case implied $495/$16.5 = 30.0x. Peers\n    (Rheinmetall ~34x, European defense avg ~22-30x, L3Harris ~18-20x)\n    [Estimate \u2014 not individually verified]. ESLT base-case deserves a\n    premium for growth, but 30x is the HIGH side of comps.\n  \u25cf EV/EBITDA: 2026E EBITDA \u2248 931 + 200 D&A + ~30 interest \u2248\n    1,160; current EV \u2248 $34.2B \u2192 29.5x. Base-case EV $22.7B \u2192 19.6x.\n  \u25cf EV/Sales: current 3.75x vs. defense comp median ~2.0x [Estimate].\n  \u21d2 Multiples analysis confirms: market price implies either WACC ~8% AND\n  super-cycle growth >10%, or ~40% overvaluation. The DCF does not\n  support the price.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive Response:\n  If ESLT scales successfully into European munitions/EW/night-vision\n  capacity, reaction from Rheinmetall, Leonardo, Thales, and Rafael/IAI\n  (Israel): capacity races, price pressure in commoditized munitions.\n  Expected impact: \u22122 on my margin trajectory if the race triggers pricing\n  concessions. Partially mitigated by regulatory allocations and\n  government-directed supply.\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity:\n  POSITIVE: high share price + $32B backlog \u2192 SBC attract talent, M&A\n  currency, trust with NATO customers for long-term supply.\n  NEGATIVE: if shares fall below $500, talent dilution risk increases and\n  US/EU customers may question reliability during geopolitical stress.\n  The 41% insider ownership anchors a moderate negative reflexivity.\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer Behavior:\n  Early-adopter phase (Israel, US special programs) \u2192 now MATINSTREAM in\n  Europe (the \"one-time favorable project mix\" is an early-mainstream\n  signal). Expect LTV stable (government contracts are 10-20-year\n  platforms), but margins will trend DOWN toward fixed-price contract\n  norms if EU procurement auctions intensify.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN UPDATED)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nPriors: base rates from L1.5. Update evidence: Q1/Q2 2026 EPS beats,\nrecord backlog, capacity expansion, Pillar II tax headwind, US-Iran\nceasefire collapse.\n\nScenario A \u2014 BEAR (25%):\n  Growth 8% in FY2026-27, then 4-5%; margins stall at 9%; WACC 9.5%\n  (geopolitical escalation / recession / defense-budget peak).\n  FCFs: 590, 650, 700, 750, 800. TV at g=3.5%.\n  IV = ~$340.\n  Path to $340: Iran war expands, Israel targeted, European budgets\n  plateau, multiple compresses to 20x EPS.\n\nScenario B \u2014 BASE (50%):\n  DCF base case, IV = $495.\n  Path: mid-teens growth 2026-27, 8% thereafter, margin expansion to\n  11.5%, no major regional escalation beyond current ceasefires.\n\nScenario C \u2014 BULL (25%):\n  European/NATO rearmament is a 10-year cycle; growth 17% / 15% / 12% /\n  10% / 9%; op margin 12.5% by 2030; WACC 8.0% (defense beta re-rates\n  down as \"secular growth\").\n  FCFs: 700, 950, 1,200, 1,450, 1,600; TV at g=4.5%.\n  IV = ~$760.\n  Path: U.S.-Iran war catalyzes NATO 5% GDP commitment; ESLT becomes a\n  principal European supplier.\n\nStep 4-3. Bayesian Posterior:\n  P(Bear) = 25%; P(Base) = 50%; P(Bull) = 25%. Updated from priors due\n  to: +evidence from backlog/margin (raise Base from 45%), \u2212evidence\n  from 2025 share issuance and US project mix (raise Bear from 20%).\n\nStep 4-4. Expected Price & Margin of Safety:\n  Expected Price = 0.25\u00d7340 + 0.50\u00d7495 + 0.25\u00d7760 = $522.5\n  50% CI: $450 \u2013 $620 [Estimate]\n  80% CI: $360 \u2013 $730 [Estimate]\n  MoS = (522.5 \u2212 741.33) / 522.5 = **\u221241.9% \u2192 NO MARGIN OF SAFETY**\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 4.5-1/2. Market implied horizon:\n  Current price ($741) implies the market is underwriting 5+ years of\n  10%+ growth and 11-12% margins \u2014 i.e., a full rearmament super-cycle\n  with flawless execution. My base DCF horizon is 3-5 years. The market\n  price implies my BULL case. There is NO arbitrage spread for a\n  3-5-year investor; the spread is \u22121.5 to \u22122 years (market is >2\n  years AHEAD of my base case). No arbitrage opportunity.\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nStep 5-1. Conviction Score (max 15):\n  1. Business quality: 3/3 (wide moat, 14/21, widening)\n  2. Valuation: 0/3 (negative MoS, no margin of safety)\n  3. Macro alignment: 2/3 (defense tailwind, neutral-to-supportive;\n     rate headwind)\n  4. Thesis clarity: 3/3 (clear KPIs: backlog, margin, FCF conversion)\n  5. Execution traceability: 3/3 (beats, record cash flow, guidance)\n  TOTAL: 11/15 \u2192 HIGH conviction in the THESIS.\n  Step 0-3 adjustment: \u22120.5 for C\u2192B transition uncertainty \u2192 10.5/15\n  (still High).\n  Step 5-2. Conviction decay: not applicable (new analysis).\n\nStep 5-3. Base Sizing (from conviction):\n  Very High (13-15) \u2192 8-12%; High (10-12) \u2192 5-8%. BUT rules impose\n  caps for macro headwinds and negative margin of safety. Because\n  valuation entered the score as 0, the MAXIMUM initial weight is\n  capped at 2% of the portfolio. If the price corrects into the\n  $450-560 zone (50% CI lower bound), sizing can expand to 4-6%.\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf Short interest: 249,525 shares / 0.91% of float; MoM +30.3% \u2014 shorts\n  are small but building [Actual]. Days to cover 3.19 \u2192 no squeeze risk.\n\u25cf Institutional ownership: 23.04% [Actual \u2014 13F, lagged] \u2014 LOW versus\n  large-cap peers \u2192 potential passive inflows if it enters more indices;\n  but also means less stable holder base.\n\u25cf Insider ownership: 41.23% [Actual] \u2014 very high alignment; reduces free\n  float; some indices exclude.\n\u25cf Options positioning: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not available in pack].\n\u25cf Sentiment: Stock is \u221227% off 52-week high ($1,016.06) despite record\n  fundamentals; sell-side mean target $846 (median $862) [Actual \u2014\n  aggregator 2026-08-18] \u2014 street is bullish, but the recent fall\n  indicates momentum had been broken.\n\u25cf Crowding risk score: 4/15 (low) \u2014 no short squeeze, low institutional\n  crowding, some retail/momentum vulnerability.\n  Position-size modifier: none required.\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf Defense-electronics exposure is a genuine diversifier vs. a typical\n  equity core (beta negative per vendor, but I estimate 0.85 forward\n  beta with meaningful tail-correlation in geopolitical crises).\n\u25cf If the portfolio already holds UK/EU defense names (BAE, Rheinmetall,\n  Thales), ESLT adds Israel-specific sovereign risk WITHOUT distinct\n  industrial exposure \u2192 do not double down.\n\u25cf Recommended: 0-2% strategic weight as a geopolitical hedge; if\n  portfolio has no defense exposure, a 2% starter is acceptable after\n  the price corrects into the fair-value zone.\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf Expected return (12-mo) from $741.33 to expected price $522.5 =\n  \u221229.5% (excluding dividends ~0.5%).\n\u25cf Probability-weighted compounded edge is NEGATIVE.\n\u25cf Kelly fraction = edge / variance \u2248 **negative \u2192 NO POSITION**.\n\u25cf If compelling reasons force a choice between conviction 10.5/15 (5-8%)\n  and Kelly (0%), the model CAPS at 0-2%, and only as a non-equation\n  geopolitical hedge overlay. Layer 5 vs Layer 6.5 gap > 20pp \u2192 resolved\n  by imposing the LOWER (Kelly) constraint. Re-analysis confirms this\n  is a value gap, not an inconsistency.\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nCatalysts (12-24 months):\n\u25cf Continued Europe order flow: backlog >$36B by end-2027.\n\u25cf Margin expansion above 10.5% non-GAAP op margin by FY2027.\n\u25cf FCF conversion >100% for 2 consecutive years; reduction of the\n  working-capital drag.\n\u25cf Directed-energy high-power laser contracts (helicopter/fighter pod) \u2014\n  second order book.\n\u25cf US elections/budgets: consistent support for Ukraine/Israel plus\n  domestic re-supply.\n\nRisks (12-36 months):\n\u25cf GEOPOLITICAL: US-Iran war resumed (actual event as of July 2026\n  [Actual \u2014 Q2 release \"hostilities\", MOU collapse]). Escalation to\n  Israel \u2192 direct operational risk and sovereign risk.\n\u25cf POLITICAL/BUDGET: NATO budget commitments roll back after 2028;\n  European orders slow; revenue growth reverts to 4-5%.\n\u25cf OPERATIONAL: Fixed-price contracts could compress margin if\n  commodity/labor inflation persists; Q2 2026 America \"favorable\"\n  mix is NOT repeatable (one-time).\n\u25cf FINANCIAL: Israeli shekel appreciation vs USD strengthens OPEX; OECD\n  Pillar II tax rate normalization (~16-17%) compresses EPS growth\n  versus the low-rate years.\n\u25cf DILUTION: FY2025 +4.9% net share issuance [Actual \u2014 Arithmetic on\n  wtd-avg shares] without disclosed cause [Unconfirmed]. If the pattern\n  continues (issuing for capacity/acqui-hires), per-share value will lag\n  enterprise value.\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf Tail correlation: ESLT correlates strongly with (a) energy prices\n  (Iran crisis), (b) Israeli sovereign CDS, (c) European defense\n  sentiment. In a 2020-style liquidity event or 2022-style rates shock,\n  correlation to SPX historically rises toward 0.6-0.8 [Estimate].\n\u25cf Conditional VaR (5% worst-case monthly): \u221225% to \u221235% during\n  escalation episodes; \u221215% in a normal rate shock [Estimate].\n\u25cf If portfolio is long US defense primes, ESLT adds less diversifying\n  benefit than standalone would suggest.\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES (THESIS INTEGRITY)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf KPI 1: Backlog \u2014 maintain >$32B and progress to >$36B by FY2027.\n  Threshold: if backlog drops below $28B, thesis de-rates; re-evaluate.\n\u25cf KPI 2: Non-GAAP operating margin \u2014 10.4% (Q2 2026) must be the floor;\n  if it drops below 9.5%, margin thesis fails.\n\u25cf KPI 3: FCF conversion \u2014 FY2026 FCF should exceed $600M; FY2027 >$800M.\n  If conversion <75% for 2 years, IV falls toward $400.\n\u25cf KPI 4: Book-to-burn ratio \u2014 must stay \u22651.0x quarterly.\n\u25cf KPI 5: Working capital \u2014 WC/Revenue should normalize at <20%; if it\n  exceeds 25%, the growth is eating cash (similar to 2023-24 pattern).\n\u25cf KPI 6: Geopolitical trigger \u2014 renewed full-scale Israel-Iran war \u2192\n  downgrade Bear to 40%, target re-rating.\n\u25cf Step 7-4. Pre-Mortem (5 years from now, this failed. Why?):\n  1. ESCALATION: A two-front war (Iran + Hezbollah) closed Israeli\n     plants for 6+ months; supply chain disrupted; US orders diverted\n     to Thales/L3Harris. Warning: repeated air-raid alerts, deaths of\n     key Israeli program leaders, insurance of plants.\n  2. EUROPE RECESSION: NATO defense spending peaked in 2027; EU fiscal\n     crisis redirected budgets; ESLT's capacity expansion became a\n     fixed-cost burden. Warning: German/French defense budget cuts in\n     any annual budget cycle, NATO GDP% targets rolled back.\n  3. TECHNOLOGICAL DISRUPTION: cheap, expendable AI-drones/inflatable\n     decoys obsoleted high-cost EW/UAV platforms; competitors (Anduril,\n     Turkish) undercut by 40%+. Warning: sustained share loss in US\n     department of defense contracts, gross margin <22%.\n\u25cf Step 7-5. Decision Journal: T0 price $741.33; IV base $495; expected\n  price $522.5; MoS \u221242%; key assumption: WACC 8.5%, g=4.0%, FY2027\n  growth 14%. Red-team note: \"the market is paying for the bull case\n  without the margin of safety.\"\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM / ADVERSARIAL (SHORT THESIS)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf SHORT THESIS:\n  1. ESLT trades at 44.9x 2026E non-GAAP EPS, 29.5x EV/EBITDA \u2014 above\n     all large-cap defense peers; any execution stumble creates a\n     30-40% de-rating.\n  2. Israel is in a permanent state of active conflict; 23% of revenue\n     is Israel-domiciled [Estimate from ~73% outside]; the stock is a\n     leveraged option on a region that keeps escalating (US-Iran war\n     resumed July 2026 [Actual]).\n  3. Fixed-price defense contracts cap margin upside; the \"one-time\n     favorable project mix\" in America is a quality-of-earnings flag.\n  4. Working-capital intensity: FY2025 \u0394WC consumed $715M [Arithmetic];\n     record revenue growth is not yet converting to sustainable FCF \u2014\n     FY2023 FCF was \u2212$73M.\n  5. Share count +4.9% in FY2025 unexplained [Unconfirmed]; if dilution\n     funds capacity, per-share FCF growth may be half the revenue growth.\n  6. The $32B backlog includes rest-of-world orders that can slip;\n     only 42% is scheduled through 2027; the other 58% is lumpy and\n     years away.\n  7. Insider ownership 41% could tempt inside sales at highs; no recent\n     buys at $740.\n\n\u25cf LOGIC COMBAT (Long vs Short):\n  Long: Moat/widening backlog, 3-quarter EPS beats, margin expansion,\n  FCF improving.\n  Short: Valuation extreme, geopolitical tail risk, working-capital\n  drag, dilution, one-time quality flags.\n  Score: Long 7/10; Short 7/10. Score gap < 2 \u2192 **conviction downgrade\n  by one full level**: final conviction MEDIUM (6-7/15) for entry at\n  current price; HIGH thesis, LOW entry.\n\n\u25cf ADVERSARIAL DATA:\n  No Hindenburg-type report exists for ESLT. Regulatory/legal exposure\n  flagged in the 20-F [Actual \u2014 Section 11, \"regulatory and legal\n  exposure\"] is standard defense-contractor risk.\n\n\u25cf KILLED ARGUMENTS:\n  \u2022 \"Negative beta = low risk\" \u2192 KILLED: beta window unknown; Israel\n    geopolitics makes 0.85 forward beta more reasonable.\n  \u2022 \"Backlog always converts to revenue at 100%\" \u2192 KILLED partially:\n    historically conversion ~70-85% due to slips [Estimate].\n  \u2022 \"Insider ownership = alignment always\" \u2192 ONLY if they aren't\n    selling; we have no insider-sell data.\n  These arguments become valid again if: share price < $500; backlog\n  conversion >1.0 for 4 quarters; insiders buy at current levels.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n\u25cf A. Methodology: PASS \u2014 Engine 1 selected with justification; explicit\n  forecast, WACC derivation (8.5% central), cross-check via multiples\n  and sensitivity.\n\u25cf B. Valuation: PASS with caveat \u2014 all key outputs tagged; no single\n  data tunnel; multi-year forecast; DCF + multiples + scenario.\n\u25cf C. B