efense-budget cycle.\n\u2022 Valuation engine: Blended \u2014 Engine 1 (70%) + Engine 2 (30%) to reflect backlog-cyclicality.\n\u2022 FCFF definition: NOPAT + D&A \u2212 capex \u2212 \u0394WC; SBC treated as expense (deducted from value via NOPAT below OI).\n\u2022 Data tags used: [Actual] = company/regulator/filing; [Estimate] = third-party/consensus; [Assumption] = model input; [Unconfirmed] = could not verify.\n\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1-1. Macro variables:\n\u2022 10Y UST: 4.71\u20134.74% (Aug 21, 2026) [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics/Fred DGS10]. Up ~44bp YoY.\n\u2022 Real rates: positive, ~1.1\u20131.3% on 10Y TIPS [Assumption from nominal/TIPS spread; exact TIPS quote [Unconfirmed]].\n\u2022 DXY: [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved. Directional assumption: rangebound-to-firm [Assumption].\n\u2022 Fed stance: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved]. Assumed on-hold after 2025 easing cycle [Assumption].\n\u2022 Global liquidity: neutral-to-tightening; defense budgets are sovereign-priority spending, less liquidity-sensitive than tech [Assumption].\n\u2022 Economic cycle: late expansion, US. Inflation ~2.5\u20133% sticky [Assumption].\n\u2022 Credit spreads: not retrieved [Unconfirmed]. Corporate IG spreads assumed ~90\u2013110bp [Assumption].\n\u2022 NATO/Europe defense: NATO allies committed to 5% of GDP on defense by 2035 (2025 Hague summit) [Actual \u2014 NATO.int]; EU rearmament accelerating.\n\nStep 1-2. Regime probability distribution:\n\u2022 Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 30%\n\u2022 Reflation (high rates + growth): 40%\n\u2022 Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 20%\n\u2022 Recession (low rates + low growth): 10%\nJustification: rates at 4.7% with solid nominal growth; defense spending is the most visible fiscal growth engine in the West, but sticky inflation keeps rates high.\n\nStep 1-3. ESLT macro sensitivity (\u22123 to +3):\n\u2022 Interest rates: \u22121 (defense is sovereign-budget driven; rate cost minor)\n\u2022 Dollar: +1 (USD revenues, partially ILS cost base; ILS weakness aids margins)\n\u2022 Liquidity: \u22121 (defense funded by bonds/taxes, not credit markets)\n\u2022 Economic cycle: +1 (geopolitics dominate cycle; mild cyclicality)\n\u2022 Commodities: +1 (energy, ammunition metals; modest input-cost exposure)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: Regime-weighted expected impact = +0.4 (mild tailwind).\nMacro Alignment Rule: Tailwind \u21d2 Conviction +0.5 steps. (Applied in Section 5.)\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates for similar enterprises:\n\u2022 Defense-electronics prime 5-year survival: ~95% [Estimate \u2014 sovereign strategic dependence]\n\u2022 5-year survival for Israeli defense exporter: ~85\u201390% [Assumption]\n\u2022 Defense prime sustaining mid-teens revenue growth for 5 consecutive years: ~35\u201350% [Estimate \u2014 historical NATO/US budget cycles]\n\u2022 Large backlog (\u22653x revenue) converting to profit without margin decay: ~60\u201370% [Assumption]\n\u2022 M&A/expansion program (capacity build) achieving ROIC > WACC: ~40% [Estimate \u2014 industry base]\n\u2022 Aerospace/defense cyclical peak \u2192 40% drawdown within 3 years: ~30\u201340% [Assumption \u2014 RS2 reference]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate:\nMy base case (mid-teens growth for 3 years, then 6\u20138%, 10.5% OPM) is MORE conservative than the market. Justifications for deviating above the \"sustained mid-teens\" base rate (I only extend ~3 years, not 5):\n(1) Backlog of $32.0B = 4.0x FY2025 revenue, 42% executable through 2027 [Actual \u2014 company]\n(2) NATO 5%-of-GDP commitment by 2035 [Actual \u2014 NATO] \u2014 a treaty-level demand shift, not a cycle\n(3) Q2-2026 GAAP OI margin 9.6% vs 8.0% YoY, non-GAAP 10.4% [Actual \u2014 company] \u2014 margin inflection is live\nI do not deviate for 5+ years: post-2028 growth falls to 6\u20138% (mature base + budget normalization).\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check:\nMarket-implied success (30%+ growth, 12%+ OPM for a decade) exceeds base-rate by >2.0x \u21d2 RED FLAG. This is the core of my bearish valuation.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding:\nESLT designs and integrates defense-electronics systems (C4I/cyber, ISTAR/EW, land/ammunition, aerospace/UAV, night vision) for Israel, the US, Europe, and Asia-Pacific. It is a Tier-1 platform provider with a $32B backlog, competing with L3Harris, Thales, BAE, and Rheinmetall. Understandability: High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0\u20133 each):\n\u2022 Intangible assets (brand, patents, directed-energy IP): 2\n\u2022 Switching costs (long platform lifecycles, integration): 3\n\u2022 Network effects (limited; some via ecosystem): 1\n\u2022 Cost advantage (Israeli R&D base, government support): 2\n\u2022 Economies of scale: 2\n\u2022 Regulatory barriers (export licenses, security clearances): 3\n\u2022 Data moat (EW/operational data, AI training): 2\nTotal: 15 / 21 \u2192 Wide Moat. Direction: Widening (backlog share gains, directed-energy leadership, US border contracts, capacity investment) [Actual/Estimate \u2014 company + press].\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification:\nFast Grower with cyclical characteristics. Core checkpoint: growth durability \u2014 good via backlog; margin quality \u2014 improving but not yet proven >10% OPM for 3 straight years.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard (FY2025 unless noted):\n\u2022 Revenue growth: 3-yr CAGR 11.2% [Filed]; FY2025 +16.3% [Filed]\n\u2022 Gross margin: 24.4% FY25; 25.3% Q2-26 GAAP [Actual]\n\u2022 Operating margin: 8.5% FY25; 9.6% Q2-26 GAAP; median 12-yr 7.9% [Filed]\n\u2022 EPS growth: FY25 $12.75 (trailing, diluted); 2026E consensus $17.07 (+33.9%) [Estimate]\n\u2022 ROE: 15.2% [Aggregator]\n\u2022 ROIC (GAAP close): NOPAT ~$536M / avg IC ~$4.7B \u2248 11.4% [Arithmetic/Estimate]\n\u2022 FCF margin: FY25 7.0% [Filed]; but FY24\u2013FY25 cumulative \u0394WC \u2212$1.075B consumed cash [Arithmetic]\n\u2022 FCF conversion: FY25 OCF $778M; FCF $553M; \u2212SBC $26M \u2192 real FCF $527M [Filed]\n\u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: net cash ~$0.43\u20130.72B depending on lease treatment (see Section 2.5)\nJudgment: Good \u2014 balance sheet solid; cash conversion is the weak point.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard:\n1. Buyback efficiency: 0/3 (share count +4.9% in FY25 \u2014 no buyback; dilution from equity comp [Filed])\n2. M&A track record: 1/3 (no massive impairments visible; but owned data insufficient for ROIC attribution [Estimate])\n3. Capex efficiency: 2/3 (capacity expansion $300M capex 2026 [Estimate]; IRR unproven)\n4. Rationality of mix: 2/3 (capex + R&D prioritized; dividends growing but small)\n5. Guidance accuracy: 3/3 (Q1-26 beat: EPS $3.87 vs $2.84; Q2-26 beat: non-GAAP EPS $4.14 vs $3.24 [Actual])\nInsider bonus: CEO/mgmt > 5%? Insider total 41.23% [Aggregator] +1; recent insider net buying 6mo [Unconfirmed] 0.\nCapital allocation total: 9/15 + 1 = 10/15 \u2014 Above average.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization:\nR&D ~7.0% of revenue (Q2-26: $159M/$2,287M = 7.0% [Actual]). Capitalize 50% with 4-yr straight-line: FY25-adjusted OI = $671M + $278M R&D \u2212 $209M amortization \u2248 +$69M \u2248 +0.9% margin [Assumption/Estimate].\nStep 2.5-2. SBC:\nFY25 SBC $26M [Filed]. Adjusted FCF = $553M \u2212 $26M = $527M [Filed]. Note: SBC is growing; $26M is still <3% of OI \u2014 immaterial for valuation but added back in OCF (overstatement risk acknowledged).\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases:\nFY25 operating leases $575M [Filed]. If capitalized and added to debt: Adjusted net debt = gross debt (LT $18M + current $83M + leases $575M) \u2212 cash $635M \u2212 ST deposits $181M = net debt \u2248 \u2212$140M (i.e., net cash $140M). Excluding leases: net cash \u2248 $715M. Midpoint used: +$0.43B [Arithmetic/Assumption].\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data assets:\nDefense franchise value embedded in backlog, not separable. No premium applied to avoid double-count [Assumption].\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC:\nAdjusted NOPAT \u2248 $0.60B; Adjusted Invested Capital = equity $4.13B + debt+leases $0.68B + unamortized R&D ~$1.0B \u2248 $5.8B \u21d2 Adj. ROIC \u2248 10.3% vs WACC ~9.0% \u2014 spread is positive but thin (1.3pp) [Estimate].\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (FY2025):\nNet income $534M + D&A $171M \u2212 maintenance capex ~$150M \u2212 \u0394WC $715M = \u2212$160M [Filed/Arithmetic].\nThe FY25 WC build (inventory $3.13B, receivables $3.33B, both near records) consumed all earnings. H1-2026 OCF of $517.8M [Actual] shows early conversion \u2014 but owner earnings remain the single biggest quality concern.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (Engine 1 \u00d7 70% + Engine 2 \u00d7 30%)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDiscount-rate derivation (WACC):\n\u2022 Risk-free: 4.70% [Actual \u2014 10Y UST Aug-2026]\n\u2022 Equity risk premium: 4.75% [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran July-2026 update not retrievable; within the 4.3\u20135.0% zone]\n\u2022 Fundamental beta: 0.80 [Assumption \u2014 vendor beta \u22120.27 [Aggregator] rejected as statistically unreliable]\n\u2022 Country risk premium (Israel, Moody's A2): +0.75% [Assumption \u2014 A2 rating from countryeconomy.com; [Unconfirmed] whether agencies have a negative outlook in Aug-2026]\n\u2022 Cost of equity = 4.70% + 0.80 \u00d7 4.75% + 0.75% = 9.25%\n\u2022 After-tax cost of debt: 5.0% \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.164) = 4.18% [Assumption]\n\u2022 Weights: 95/5 equity/debt (net-cash-ish balance sheet) \u21d2 WACC = 0.95 \u00d7 9.25% + 0.05 \u00d7 4.18% \u2248 9.0% (round); central DCF uses 8.5% to reflect backlog visibility [Assumption]\n\u2022 Terminal growth: 2.5% (long-run nominal GDP) [Assumption]\n\nFORECAST TABLE (Base case, $B unless noted) [All columns [Assumption] unless tagged]:\n\n| Year   | Revenue | Growth% | OPM% | OI | NOPAT (tax 16.4%) | D&A (2.15% rev) | Capex | \u0394WC | FCFF |\n|--------|---------|---------|------|-----|-------------------|-------------------|-------|------|------|\n| 2026E  | 9.13    | +14.9%  | 9.6% | 0.877 | 0.733 | 0.196 | 0.300 | 0.060 | 0.569 |\n| 2027E  | 10.22   | +12.0%  | 10.0%| 1.022| 0.854 | 0.220 | 0.320 | 0.055 | 0.699 |\n| 2028E  | 11.24   | +10.0%  | 10.5%| 1.180| 0.986 | 0.242 | 0.340 | 0.051 | 0.837 |\n| 2029E  | 12.14   | +8.0%   | 10.5%| 1.275| 1.066 | 0.261 | 0.360 | 0.045 | 0.922 |\n| 2030E  | 12.87   | +6.0%   | 10.5%| 1.351| 1.129 | 0.277 | 0.380 | 0.037 | 0.989 |\n\nRevenue sourcing: 2026 = mid-teens company guidance [Actual \u2014 call excerpt: \"mid-teens annual growth\"; consensus $9.12B [Estimate]]; 2027 = consensus $10.28B [Estimate]; 2028\u20132030 = my deceleration to 10%, 8%, 6% [Assumption \u2014 backlog extends to ~2029\u20132030, but only 42% is contractually scheduled through 2027 [Actual]].\nMargin sourcing: OPM starts at Q2-26 GAAP 9.6% [Actual]; path to 10.5% [Assumption \u2014 non-GAAP already 10.4% [Actual]; GAAP convergence over 2 years].\nTax: 16.4% [Actual \u2014 Q2-26 effective rate incl. Pillar II; normalizes up from FY24\u2013FY25 sub-10%].\nCapex: 2026 \u2248 $300M [Estimate \u2014 management plan]; thereafter $320\u2013380M = ~3% of revenue [Assumption].\n\u0394WC: normalized 5% of incremental revenue [Assumption \u2014 after FY25's $715M build; H1-26 OCF shows some release; high uncertainty flagged].\n\nTerminal value (base, WACC 8.5%, g 2.5%):\nFCF2031 = NOPAT 1.157 + D&A 0.284 \u2212 capex 0.390 \u2212 \u0394WC 0.030 \u2248 1.021\nTV = 1.021 / (0.085 \u2212 0.025) = 17.0\nPV of year-1\u20135 FCFF at 8.5%: 0.525 + 0.593 + 0.655 + 0.666 + 0.658 = 3.10\nPV of TV = 17.0 \u00d7 0.665 = 11.31\nEV = 14.41; + net cash 0.43 \u21d2 equity = 14.84; / 46.9M shares = $316/share \u2192 round base IV \u2248 $320 [Assumption-stated estimate].\n\nMultiples cross-check (Engine 1 secondary):\n\u2022 2026E non-GAAP EPS $17.07 [Estimate] at 25x = $427; at 30x = $512\n\u2022 Peer multiples: Rheinmetall ~34x, US primes ~20\u201325x (2026E) [Estimate \u2014 not fully retrieved; RHM 34x from mumak.me]\n\u2022 ESLT at 43x 2026E non-GAAP EPS implies a permanent premium over every prime.\nMultiples check: fair range $340\u2013$520 using peers.\n\nEngine 2 (cycle-adjusted) cross-check: Mid-cycle earnings power: revenue $12.0B \u00d7 10.5% OPM \u00d7 0.836 \u00d7 25x \u2248 $263\u2013$300/share. Bear-ish anchor; blended model yields final IV near $355.\n\nTornado sensitivity (top 3 variables):\n1. WACC (7.5%\u2192$590; 9.5%\u2192$190): \u00b1$260\n2. Terminal OPM (12.5%\u2192$589; 8.5%\u2192$240): \u00b1$250\n3. Revenue CAGR 2027\u20132030 (14%\u2192$440; 6%\u2192$280): \u00b1$160\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Competitive response: \u22122 (if ESLT wins large European EW/UAV programs, L3Harris/Thales/BAE will price aggressively; some profit already visible in Q2-26 aerospace \u22128% one-time mix)\n\u2022 Reflexivity: positive today \u2014 record backlog pulls talent and government co-funding (customer-funded factories [Estimate \u2014 SeekingAlpha]); negative risk in a conflict shock\n\u2022 Customer behavior: European defense procurement shifting from single-source to multi-year frameworks (5% GDP target) \u2014 favors ESLT's long-cycle model but also invites US primes into Europe.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPrior (Section 1.5) updated by Q2-26 actuals (backlog $32B, OPM 9.6%, H1 OCF $518M) \u2014 likelihood ratio >1 for margin/backlog; <1 for WC and valuation.\n\n| Scenario | Prob | WACC | g | OPM 2030 | Rev 2030 | IV/share |\n|----------|------|------|------|----------|----------|----------|\n| Bear (peace dividend / execution miss / WC persists) | 25% | 9.5% | 2.0% | 9.5% | $11.0B | $190 |\n| Base (rearmament continues; margin 10.5%) | 50% | 8.5% | 2.5% | 10.5% | $12.9B | $320 |\n| Bull (super-cycle extends; margin 12.5%) | 25% | 7.5% | 3.0% | 12.5% | $14.8B | $590 |\n\nExpected IV = 0.25(190) + 0.5(320) + 0.25(590) = $355.\nExpected value 2027 (one-year forward): bear $220 / base $380 / bull $700 \u21d2 EV \u2248 $420.\n50% CI: $280\u2013$480; 80% CI: $190\u2013$650.\nMargin of safety at $741.33 = \u2212109% \u21d2 NONE.\nPrice discipline: no new capital. Staged accumulation at \u2264$500.\n\nSECTION 4.5. HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Market-implied horizon for $741.33: reverse-DCF at 8.5% WACC requires ~$2.8B annual FCFF by 2030 \u2014 implying \u226520% FCFF margin (OPM \u2265 15%) or ~17% CAGR revenue for 10 years; sustainable OPM >+15% has never been achieved in 12 years (peak 9.4% GAAP in 2021 [Filed]). Market horizon = 10+ years of perfection.\n\u2022 My horizon: 3\u20135 years. Spread = \u22125 to \u22127 years \u21d2 NEGATIVE arbitrage. The market is paying for outcomes 5+ years out that require a structural break from ESLT's own operating history.\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nConviction score (T0):\n1. Business quality: 3/3 (wide moat, record backlog, margin inflection)\n2. Valuation attractiveness: 0/3 (MoS \u2212109%)\n3. Macro alignment: 2/3 (defense supercycle tailwind, but Middle East escalation cuts both ways)\n4. Thesis clarity: 3/3 (backlog conversion, OPM, WC/sales are measurable)\n5. Execution traceability: 2/3 (beats visible; multi-year margin target not formalized)\nTotal: 10/15. Macro tailwind +0.5 \u2192 10.5/15 \u2192 High analysis-CONVICTION, but the direction is negative.\nConviction decay: \u22121 point every 6 months without confirming new data.\nSizing guideline (High conviction): max 4\u20136% only if MoS were adequate \u2014 HERE: no position.\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Short interest: 0.91% of float, 3.19 DTC, but +30.3% MoM \u2014 rising but negligible [Aggregator]\n\u2022 Institutional ownership: 23.04% (low vs US peers); insider 41.23% \u2014 high insider alignment, but creates low free-float liquidity [Aggregator]\n\u2022 Options positioning: [Unconfirmed \u2014 not retrieved]\n\u2022 Sentiment: price \u221227% from 52-wk high ($1,016\u2192$741); analysts: 1 Strong Buy, 5 Hold, 1 Sell; avg PT $835.5 [Estimate] \u2014 analysts are anchored above price; consensus \"Hold\" rather than \"Buy\"\n\u2022 Crowding risk score: 6/15 \u2014 defense is a crowded long theme; high insider (41%) reduces ETF-driven flow relevance. Crowding penalty \u22121%p if a position existed.\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nESLT as a portfolio holding:\n\u2022 Adds defense/geopolitical beta; negative vendor beta (\u22120.27) suggests crisis-hedge properties, but the vendor beta window/benchmark is unknown [Unconfirmed]\n\u2022 Overlaps with European defense names (RHM, BAE, Thales) \u2014 do NOT add if such exposure already exists\n\u2022 Israel-country risk is a unique, non-diversifiable idiosyncratic layer (US-Iran hostilities resumed as of Aug-2026 [Actual \u2014 company risk disclosure])\n\u2022 Better expression of the same thesis with cleaner pricing: US primes (LMT, RTX) at 20\u201325x forward EPS\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nFull-Kelly: f* = [(p \u00d7 b) \u2212 q] / b, where p = 0.50, q = 0.50, b = (355 \u2212 741)/741 = \u22120.52\nf* = (0.5 \u00d7 \u22120.52 \u2212 0.5) / \u22120.52 = (\u22120.26 \u2212 0.50)/\u22120.52 = NEGATIVE.\nKelly = 0% at T0. Half-Kelly = 0%.\nSizing gap vs Layer 5 (0% vs 0%) = 0pp < 20pp \u21d2 consistent.\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nCatalysts (bull): 1) $32B backlog conversion at >10% OPM [Actual]; 2) NATO 5% GDP target \u2192 European orders re-rate backlog further [Actual \u2014 NATO]; 3) Directed-energy/UAV program wins (airborne HPL, F-35 helmet) [Actual/Estimate]; 4) WC release \u2192 FCF beats; 5) Israel-Iran de-escalation \u2192 country-risk premium compresses [Unconfirmed].\nRisks (bear): 1) OPM stall <10% GAAP; 2) \u0394WC persists at >$300M/yr (inventory $3.13B [Filed]); 3) Peace acceleration or US politics shift \u2192 European budget pause; 4) Export-control/BDS/legal escalation; 5) Pillar II tax normalization to 16\u201318% vs consensus 12\u201313%; 6) Dilution: +4.9% share count in FY25 [Filed].\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Tail correlation: ESLT's largest stress-betas align with (a) a global risk-off (WACC up, multiple compression \u2014 2022-style) and (b) an Israel-specific security shock (country risk +, ILS depreciation \u2014 partially offset by USD revenues).\n\u2022 CVaR estimate: In a 2-sigma geopolitical/defense drawdown, ESLT trades at 52-wk low $447; CVaR from $741 \u2248 \u221240% over 3\u20136 months [Estimate \u2014 52-wk range $447\u2013$1,016 as the empirical shock envelope].\n\u2022 Portfolio tail-sharing: if the portfolio already holds defense primes, the additive tail risk of ESLT is ~zero diversification benefit; correlation to European defense names estimated >0.7 in stress [Assumption].\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nThesis KPIs (must trigger re-evaluation if breached):\n1. Backlog \u2192 Revenue conversion: revenue 2026 \u2265 $9.0B and 2027 \u2265 $10.0B [guidance/consensus \u2014 miss >5% \u21d2 downgrade IV]\n2. GAAP OPM \u2265 9.6% in Q3/Q4-2026, then \u226510% by Q4-2027; non-GAAP OPM \u2265 10.4% hold [Actual anchor]\n3. \u0394WC: annual working-capital burn < $150M by FY2026; inventory/sales declining from 39% [Filed]\n4. FCF (after SBC) > $400M in FY2026 [my base 0.569 \u2212 SBC \u2248 0.54]\n5. Middle East conflict status \u2014 renewed Israel/Iran hostilities vs durable ceasefire [Actual \u2014 must track]\n6. Tax rate: if effective tax > 19%, consensus EPS $17.07 is overstated \u21d2 cut EPS [Assumption]\n7. Share count: if net dilution >1.5%/yr, deduct from per-share value\n8. Capex: if > $400M/yr, ask whether customer-funded factories offset the drag\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nShort thesis (using same data):\n1. Valuation: 43x 2026E EPS vs peers 20\u201334x; market has paid for a 10-year super-cycle that does not exist in 12 years of ESLT's own financials.\n2. Margin ceiling: GAAP OPM peaked at 9.4% in 2021 and only Q2-26 reached 9.6% \u2014 the 12%+ OPM required for $741 has no precedent.\n3. Working-capital black hole: receivables + inventory = $6.46B = 81% of FY25 revenue; FY25 \u0394WC of $715M consumed 134% of net income.\n4. Country-risk binary: US-Iran hostilities resumed at filing date; Moody's A2 with conflict recrudescence risk; any escalation hits the multiple first.\n5. Pillar II tax: Q2-26 effective tax 16.4% vs 5.6% prior year; consensus EPS likely overstated by 5\u201310%.\n6. Dilution + insider overhang: shares +4.9% in FY25; 41% insider ownership means a future supply event is possible [Filed/Unconfirmed].\n7. Backlog concentration: 42% of backlog only through 2027; growth cliff after 2028 if rearmament peaks.\n\nLong counter-arguments:\n1. Backlog $32B = 4.0x revenue; record; 73% from outside Israel \u2014 demand real.\n2. Q2-26 shows margin inflection: